  "answer": "// stronglyConnectedComponents returns the SCCs of the directed graph, using
// Kosaraju: an order by finish time on the graph, then components on its
// reverse.
func stronglyConnectedComponents(n int, edges [][]int) [][]int {
	adj := make([][]int, n)
	rev := make([][]int, n)
	for _, e := range edges {
		adj[e[0]] = append(adj[e[0]], e[1])
		rev[e[1]] = append(rev[e[1]], e[0])
	}

	// First pass: push each node after all of its out-neighbours are done.
	order := make([]int, 0, n)
	seen := make([]bool, n)
	iter := make([]int, n)
	for s := 0; s < n; s++ {
		if seen[s] {
			continue
		}
		seen[s] = true
		stack := []int{s}
		for len(stack) > 0 {
			u := stack[len(stack)-1]
			if iter[u] < len(adj[u]) {
				v := adj[u][iter[u]]
				iter[u]++
				if !seen[v] {
					seen[v] = true
					stack = append(stack, v)
				}
				continue
			}
			order = append(order, u)
			stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]
		}
	}

	// Second pass: reachability in the reverse graph, taking roots in
	// decreasing finish time.
	comps := make([][]int, 0)
	done := make([]bool, n)
	for i := len(order) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
		s := order[i]
		if done[s] {
			continue
		}
		done[s] = true
		comp := []int{s}
		for j := 0; j < len(comp); j++ {
			u := comp[j]
			for _, v := range rev[u] {
				if !done[v] {
					done[v] = true
					comp = append(comp, v)
				}
			}
		}
		comps = append(comps, comp)
	}
	return comps
}",
  "explanation": "This is Kosaraju. First pass: a depth-first traversal of the graph that appends each node only after all of its out-neighbours are finished, producing an order by finish time. Second pass: take nodes in decreasing finish time and, from each unclaimed one, collect everything reachable in the reversed graph. That set is exactly one component, because reachability in the graph intersected with reachability in the reverse is mutual reachability, and the finish order guarantees you start at a node whose component sits at the top of the remaining condensation, so the reverse search cannot leak into a component that was already taken. Tarjan's single-pass lowlink algorithm is the other standard answer and is equally linear. Note the traversal here is an explicit stack with a per-node iterator index: recursion would be fine on paper but the test graph is 300000 nodes deep in the worst case.",
  "hints": [
   "Mutual reachability means reachable forward and reachable backward, so keep the reversed adjacency too.",
   "Order matters: the first pass exists only to decide which node to start the second pass from.",
   "In the first pass, record a node after its out-neighbours are exhausted, not when you first see it."
  ],
  "refs": [
   "LC 1192 Critical Connections in a Network",
   "LC 2101 Detonate the Maximum Bombs"
  ]
 },
